20260527.113312.183 [>kpop..........] ## Communication
20260527.113312.183 [>kpop..........] 
20260527.113312.183 [>kpop..........] ## Definition: Claims vs Hypotheses
20260527.113312.183 [>kpop..........] 
20260527.113312.183 [>kpop..........] - Label uncertain reasoning as Hypothesis; only use Claim with explicit evidence.
20260527.113312.183 [>kpop..........] - Claims must cite evidence (code refs, logs, metrics). Otherwise, downgrade to Hypothesis.
20260527.113312.183 [>kpop..........] - For each Hypothesis, include:
20260527.113312.183 [>kpop..........]   - Hypothesis: concise, falsifiable statement.
20260527.113312.183 [>kpop..........]   - Predictions: measurable outcomes if true.
20260527.113312.183 [>kpop..........]   - Test: minimal experiment (setup, variables, metrics, pass/fail).
20260527.113312.183 [>kpop..........]   - Confounders: likely alternatives and controls.
20260527.113312.183 [>kpop..........] - Language:
20260527.113312.183 [>kpop..........]   - Hypothesis: “suggests”, “may”, “indicates”.
20260527.113312.183 [>kpop..........]   - Claim (with evidence): “shows”, “demonstrates”, “causes”.
20260527.113312.183 [>kpop..........] - Label any statement which is a hypothesis as such.
20260527.113312.183 [>kpop..........] - The use plan,`./.malvin/logs/20260527_153303_0hnrmmal/request.md`, when present, overrides ADVICE. ADVICE is not binding.
20260527.113312.183 [>kpop..........] 
20260527.113312.183 [>kpop..........] ## Shorthand
20260527.113312.183 [>kpop..........] 
20260527.113312.183 [>kpop..........] - DCC: Don't Change Code
20260527.113312.183 [>kpop..........] - RL: Be sure to look at recent logs.
20260527.113312.183 [>kpop..........] 
20260527.113312.183 [>kpop..........] ## Style
20260527.113312.183 [>kpop..........] 
20260527.113312.183 [>kpop..........] When communicating to the user:
20260527.113312.184 [>kpop..........] 
20260527.113312.184 [>kpop..........] - No corporate-speak (e.g., "learnings", "close the loop")
20260527.113312.184 [>kpop..........] - No cheezy dev-speak (e.g., "bolt that on", "fire-and-forget", "duct tape")
20260527.113312.184 [>kpop..........] - No colloquialisms
20260527.113312.184 [>kpop..........] - Write in clear, plain language.
20260527.113312.184 [>kpop..........] - Use complete sentences.
20260527.113312.184 [>kpop..........] 
20260527.113312.184 [>kpop..........] # Repo Coding Rules
20260527.113312.184 [>kpop..........] 
20260527.113312.184 [>kpop..........] Work until the end without asking for user input. If you are uncertain about an implementation
20260527.113312.184 [>kpop..........] detail, use your best judgement. There will always be an opportunity to revise later on.
20260527.113312.184 [>kpop..........] 
20260527.113312.184 [>kpop..........] The Quality Gates section lists the exact shell commands from `.malvin/checks` (non-empty lines, in order). Malvin creates that file with layout-appropriate defaults when it is missing. Use that list as the source of truth for which languages and checks apply. Do NOT add a language that is not already present in the project.
20260527.113312.184 [>kpop..........] 
20260527.113312.184 [>kpop..........] ## Quality Gates
20260527.113312.184 [>kpop..........] 
20260527.113312.184 [>kpop..........] Be sure that all applicable checks pass.
20260527.113312.184 [>kpop..........] Malvin writes quality-gate command output to `./.malvin/logs/20260527_153303_0hnrmmal/quality_gates.log`; inspect that log when you need failure details.
20260527.113312.184 [>kpop..........] 
20260527.113312.184 [>kpop..........] - `kiss check`
20260527.113312.184 [>kpop..........] - `ruff check .`
20260527.113312.184 [>kpop..........] - `pytest -sv tests`
20260527.113312.184 [>kpop..........] 
20260527.113312.184 [>kpop..........] When presented with failures or violations, respond to them earnestly. Improve the code in a way that respects the spirit of the quality-gate feedback.
20260527.113312.184 [>kpop..........] 
20260527.113312.184 [>kpop..........] When you want to iterate quickly on a bit of code, try these fast tools:
20260527.113312.184 [>kpop..........] 
20260527.113312.184 [>kpop..........] - `cargo check` - Tells whether code *would* compile, but doesn't compile it.
20260527.113312.184 [>kpop..........] - `kiss test <filename>` - Runs just the subset of Rust or Python tests that test a given filename. Heuristic but fast b/c you aren't running all tests.
20260527.113312.184 [>kpop..........] 
20260527.113312.184 [>kpop..........] `ruff check` and `kiss check` are also very fast, even when applied to the entire codebase.
20260527.113312.184 [>kpop..........] 
20260527.113312.184 [>kpop..........] ## Tips and Soft Requirements
20260527.113312.184 [>kpop..........] 
20260527.113312.184 [>kpop..........] - Run `kiss rules` before getting started so that you can avoid `kiss` VIOLATIONs.
20260527.113312.184 [>kpop..........] - Run `kiss --help` to familiarize yourself with kiss's coding tools, like `test` and `mv`. `kiss test` can be especially helpful in reducing the unit test burden in a coding loop.
20260527.113312.184 [>kpop..........] - Do not write "documentation parity guards". Do not write comments.
20260527.113312.184 [>kpop..........] - Keep each unit test's running time under 10 seconds (<1s would be great).
20260527.113312.184 [>kpop..........] - Write code to fail fast. Assert liberally. DRY.
20260527.113312.184 [>kpop..........] - Don't name source code files ".inc". .rs and .py are the correct extensions.
20260527.113312.185 [>kpop..........] - Keep code consistent with `./.malvin/logs/20260527_153303_0hnrmmal/request.md` when this run includes a plan file.
20260527.113312.185 [>kpop..........] - Use (up to 4) parallel subagents whereven possible.
20260527.113312.185 [>kpop..........] - Do not write tests for tests. That's silly overkill.
20260527.113312.185 [>kpop..........] - In tests, avoid doing things that take a long or highly-variable amount of time. Also avoid external services if at all possible. They can be unreliable, and the operators might get annoyed if we keep using them just for our tests.
20260527.113312.185 [>kpop..........] 
20260527.113312.185 [>kpop..........] ## Nota Bene & Hard Requirements
20260527.113312.185 [>kpop..........] 
20260527.113312.185 [>kpop..........] ALL checks and tests should pass on ALL    files (not just the ones you modified). Don't tell me
20260527.113312.185 [>kpop..........]  about "pre-existing" problems. We're here to work. To fix. Be tenacious and intrepid. There's no excuse
20260527.113312.185 [>kpop..........]  for not getting ALL checks and tests to pass on ALL files.
20260527.113312.185 [>kpop..........] Do not modify protected workspace files: `.kissconfig`, `.kissignore`, `.malvin/checks`, or `.malvin/config.toml`. Malvin snapshots them before agent work and restores them after each coder step, so edits to those files will not stick; fix violations in application code instead. Don't call `kiss clamp` or `kiss mimic`. You can't get out of it. You need to restructure the code to satisfy `kiss check`.
20260527.113312.185 [>kpop..........] Once in a while, `kiss check` will show *many* violations. Don't get overwhelmed or intimidated by that. Keep your composure. Maybe use your TODO list. Maybe make a plan. Remember that a journey of a thousand miles begins with a single step.
20260527.113312.185 [>kpop..........] Don't add `# noqa` except to ensure correct functioning of the code.
20260527.113312.185 [>kpop..........] Don't cheat the tests. Make earnest attempts to pass the linters and unit tests in the spirit
20260527.113312.185 [>kpop..........]  in which they were designed.
20260527.113312.185 [>kpop..........] Your task is to get ALL checks and tests to pass on ALL files.
20260527.113312.185 [>kpop..........] Do NOT create Rust code in a Python-only project, or Python code in a Rust-only project.
20260527.113312.185 [>kpop..........] DO NOT add, commit, or otherwise perform a modifying operation on git. (You may inspect the log or diff to files, but no changing git.)
20260527.113312.185 [>kpop..........] 
20260527.113312.185 [>kpop..........] # Definition: KPop
20260527.113312.185 [>kpop..........] 
20260527.113312.185 [>kpop..........] [
20260527.113312.185 [>kpop..........] KPop is short for "Karl Popper".
20260527.113312.185 [>kpop..........] You may or may not be solving a repo coding problem, so the Repo Coding Rules may or may not apply.
20260527.113312.185 [>kpop..........] KPop may be referenced later on like a command, "KPop: <problem statement or question>"
20260527.113312.185 [>kpop..........] ]
20260527.113312.185 [>kpop..........] 
20260527.113312.185 [>kpop..........] Apply this method to the user's problem.
20260527.113312.185 [>kpop..........] 
20260527.113312.185 [>kpop..........] Restate the problem clearly.
20260527.113312.185 [>kpop..........] 
20260527.113312.185 [>kpop..........] Repeat until you think you've solved the problem:
20260527.113312.185 [>kpop..........] LOOP_START
20260527.113312.185 [>kpop..........] 
20260527.113312.185 [>kpop..........] - **Brainstorm**: Optionally, if you want creative ideas, run `malvin invent IDEAS_PROMPT`, where you specify the `IDEAS_PROMPT`. Call this on at least one iteration. It might take about 30s to return, but I promise it'll be worth the wait. Use these ideas to help generate new hypotheses.
20260527.113312.185 [>kpop..........] - **Hypothesize**: Hypothesize one falsifiable explanation of the cause of the problem.
20260527.113312.185 [>kpop..........] - **Predict**: Define a falsifying test. If the hypothesis were true, what outcome would the test produce?
20260527.113312.185 [>kpop..........] - **Falsify**: Run the test. If falsified, reject the hypothesis.
20260527.113312.186 [>kpop..........] 
20260527.113312.186 [>kpop..........] LOOP_END
20260527.113312.186 [>kpop..........] 
20260527.113312.186 [>kpop..........] Log your hypotheses and test results -- as they become available -- to `./.malvin/logs/20260527_153303_0hnrmmal/_kpop/exp_log_20260527_153303_0hnrmmal.md` (never a repo-root `./_kpop/` path). Do not write KPop logs under repo-root `./_kpop/`; use only `./.malvin/logs/20260527_153303_0hnrmmal/_kpop/exp_log_20260527_153303_0hnrmmal.md`.
20260527.113312.186 [>kpop..........] 
20260527.113312.186 [>kpop..........] When you are all done, append a brief executive summary and a super-brief tl;dr to the log, and echo both to the user (the chat/context) directly.
20260527.113312.186 [>kpop..........] 
20260527.113312.186 [>kpop..........] # This KPOP turn
20260527.113312.186 [>kpop..........] 
20260527.113312.186 [>kpop..........] Complete up to `10` KPOP iterations in this turn. Append each iteration as a section `## Step K — KPOP …` to `./.malvin/logs/20260527_153303_0hnrmmal/_kpop/exp_log_20260527_153303_0hnrmmal.md`; use the next integer K that is not already used in that file.
20260527.113312.186 [>kpop..........] 
20260527.113312.186 [>kpop..........] If you solve the problem before using the iterations budget, append a section `## KPOP_SOLVED` with one short paragraph explaining why, and stop adding new steps.
